 \section{Introduction and Positioning}
-Can a fly-connectome spiking neural network retain collision avoidance after connection loss? The task outcome depends on the damaged network's interaction with sensing, feedback and delayed actuation. Two control laws can complete the same healthy tasks yet respond differently to matched damage. Assessing this response requires both collision outcomes and executable checks that healthy and damaged runs form the intended pairs over the declared observation horizon.
+How do matched connection losses in a connectome-derived spiking neural network (SNN) affect collision outcomes as feedback, observation horizon and calibrated sensing change? Healthy binary success and early neural activity describe different aspects of this question. A task-level assessment must retain the pairing between damaged and healthy runs and identify when first contact occurs.
 
-Connectome-derived models predict sensorimotor outputs\cite{shiu2024drosophila}. An reports task- and state-dependent function preservation under sparsification\cite{an2026sparse}; Jo compares circuit and virtual-arena readouts after connectome damage\cite{jo2026simulating}. Closed-loop fault evaluation is also established: DriveFI searches faults and scenarios that violate autonomous-driving safety constraints\cite{jha2019drivefi}. SpikeFI, SpikingJET and enpheeph provide configurable SNN fault campaigns\cite{spyrou2024spikefi,gogebakan2024spikingjet,colucci2022enpheeph}. These precedents motivate measuring the complete damage-to-task chain, rather than claiming a new fault-injection principle.
+Shiu et al.\cite{shiu2024drosophila} establish connectome-derived LIF sensorimotor predictions; here the output drives a synthetic pursuit--escape plant. An\cite{an2026sparse} studies task- and state-dependent preservation under sparsification, and Jo\cite{jo2026simulating} couples damaged circuits to virtual-arena behavior. Our comparison instead targets paired geometric first contact under two frozen feedback laws and a declared horizon. Our warning target is subsequent contact from a 400-ms prefix, rather than identification of a damaged network. These are distinct evaluation questions, not claims that task dependence or neural/behavioral dissociation is new.
 
-CRN synchronization\cite{glasserman1992crn}, hybrid-trajectory monitoring\cite{donze2010breach} and task/resource benchmarking\cite{yik2023neurobench,dangelo2026embodied} supply established tools. Cross-layer NoC analysis connects hardware routing defects to SNN behavior\cite{chessa2026crosslayer}; our functional row deletions supply no comparable physical fault mapping. Here, the evaluated increment is system-level quantification together with executable checks of the evidence supporting it.
+DriveFI searches faults and scenarios that violate closed-loop driving safety\cite{jha2019drivefi}; SpikeFI, SpikingJET and enpheeph provide configurable SNN injection campaigns\cite{spyrou2024spikefi,gogebakan2024spikingjet,colucci2022enpheeph}. Common random numbers\cite{glasserman1992crn}, hybrid-trajectory monitoring\cite{donze2010breach}, task/resource benchmarking\cite{yik2023neurobench,dangelo2026embodied} and physical fault propagation\cite{chessa2026crosslayer} are established. Our evidence interface checks supplied paired records and native replay obligations, complementing injection and violation search.
 
-We quantify paired task loss under two frozen feedback laws and separately recalibrated, same-prior receptive-field (RF) realizations. We contribute an evidence interface that checks campaign invariants and delegates native input/plant semantics to independent replay, with tested reuse on a delayed tank. We also evaluate frozen neural warnings against later collision, retaining misses, false alarms and uncertain transfer. Together, these contributions connect conditional task outcomes to testable pairing, timing and exposure assumptions.
+We contribute three concrete findings and capabilities: (i) a paired first-contact characterization in which RF-0 proportional 50\%-deletion losses rise from 2/48 to 11/48 between 900 and 1000\,ms, with no relay contacts, alongside smaller uncertain contrasts in separately recalibrated RF cohorts; (ii) a frozen-prefix evaluation exposing 11/13 missed RF-0 contacts and cross-configuration false alarms, limiting substitution of the tested scalar warnings for task outcomes; and (iii) executable pairing, timing and exposure checks with native replay, tested error cases and limited cross-plant reuse. These quantify a mismatch between healthy success, deletion-task preservation and frozen warnings, without identifying a damage-specific protection mechanism.
